## 20. Localized-window separation of the two leading response ratios (proved)

This is the item the continuation named as the highest-alpha next theorem, and it
is now a theorem here rather than a plan.

*Analytic core* (`CoshWindowSeparation`, no project dependencies).  Write
`coshDiff x u v = cosh (x v) − cosh (x u)`.  Then for `0 < a < b`:

    sinh t < t cosh t                                         (sinh_lt_mul_cosh)
    q sinh p < p sinh q          for 0 < p < q                (mul_sinh_lt_mul_sinh)
    x cosh x sinh y < y cosh y sinh x   for 0 < x < y         (mul_cosh_mul_sinh_lt)
    sinh(bs) sinh(as') < sinh(as) sinh(bs')   for 0 < s < s'  (sinh_cross_lt)

and hence, by two applications of the Cauchy mean value theorem,

    coshDiff a c₁ c₂ · coshDiff b c₀ c₁ < coshDiff a c₀ c₁ · coshDiff b c₁ c₂
                                                              (cosh_increment_cross)

for `0 < c₀ < c₁ < c₂`.  Pure algebra then gives strict monotonicity of the
increment ratio `ρ(u,v) = coshDiff b u v / coshDiff a u v` in **each** endpoint, and
the packaged window statement `coshDiff_cross_of_window_gate`: under a gate on four
window endpoints, every pair of sample points drawn from three ordered windows
separates strictly.  The gate is not vacuous — `window_gate_of_point_windows` proves
it outright for degenerate (point) windows.

*Transport to the leading coefficients* (`LiteralWeilWindowRatioSeparation`).  The
polarised leading coefficient is the double integral of a bilinear kernel,

    covForm p q a = 3/4 ∬ p(u) q(v) (u² − v²)(cosh(au) − cosh(av)) du dv
                                                       (covForm_eq_crossIntegral)

— proved without Fubini, because the kernel is a finite sum of products of
one-dimensional moments.  For **window tapers** (nonnegative, continuous, compactly
supported, positive mass, support inside a prescribed modulus window) the pointwise
window bounds integrate to

    crossIntegral p q b · coshDiff a β₀ β₁ ≤ coshDiff b β₀ β₁ · crossIntegral p q a
    coshDiff b α₀ α₂ · crossIntegral p q a ≤ crossIntegral p q b · coshDiff a α₀ α₂
    0 < crossIntegral p q a                     (disjoint windows, positive masses)

and therefore the **cross determinant is strictly positive**:

    covForm p q₁ a · covForm p q₀ b < covForm p q₀ a · covForm p q₁ b   (covForm_det_lt)

with the quantitative refinement `crossIntegral_det_ge` and the uniform upper bound
`crossIntegral_le`.  No `W(t)`, no absolute zero mass, no tail estimate and no
numerical input occur in any of these statements.

*Literal transport* (`LiteralWeilWindowSchurAdmission`).  Since
`zeroLeadingCoeff g ρ = 2 · mult ρ · targetLeadingCoeff g (heightOf ρ)`, the literal
radius-free determinant is `4 · mult σ · mult ρ` times the determinant of the two
`targetLeadingCoeff`s (`leadingCrossDet_eq`).  For the two-window tapers
`g_j = p + λ q_j` the determinant splits **exactly** as

    det = 4 λ² · (cross determinant) + detRest        (targetLeadingCoeff_det_expand)

where every summand of `detRest` carries a *within-window* self response.  Under the
window gate and domination of `detRest`, the literal leading determinant is nonzero
(`leadingCrossDet_windowPair_ne_zero`), and a nonzero leading determinant produces
explicit radii at which the literal Gram wedge is strictly positive and the target
survives the exact Schur removal of the nuisance zero
(`exists_radius_windowPair_transverseComp_ne_zero`).

