        = 18 A log(|t|+4) / J + 72 A / √J          (`tsum_tailTermFrom_le`),

with `A` the constant of the unconditional local zero count.  The bound holds for
*every* cutoff `J ≥ 1`; it is not asymptotic.  `farShellBound_tendsto_zero` and
`exists_cutoff_tail_lt` record that the cutoff can be chosen to make the far tail
smaller than any prescribed `ε > 0`.

### 27.2 The finite signed near-shell carrier interface

`NearFarCarrierSplit` combines 27.1 with the uniform curvature constant of §26.1.  The
near shells form a genuinely finite set, `nearSet_finite` / `nearFinset t J`, because
`|Im ρ − t| < J` places the zero in one counting window.  For every compactly supported
`C²` real-even taper `g`, with `Z_ρ = zeroConeValue g t 0 ρ` and `R` the
functional-equation reflection,

    | ∑'_{|Im ρ − t| ≥ 1} (Z_ρ + Z_{Rρ}) − ∑_{ρ ∈ nearFinset t J} (Z_ρ + Z_{Rρ}) |
        ≤ C · farShellBound A |t| J          (`abs_signedFarTerm_sub_nearFinset_le`),

with `C` the uniform reflection-pair curvature constant, and the right-hand side tends
to `0` in the cutoff (`exists_near_far_split`, `exists_cutoff_near_far_split`).

So the signed far carrier is now, unconditionally and with explicit constants,

    a finite signed sum over the shells `1 ≤ |Im ρ − t| < J`
        + a remainder bounded by an explicit function of `J` that decays like
          `log(|t|)/J + 1/√J`.

### 27.2b The decomposition on the literal `D_off` object

`OffOrdinateCutoffCarrier` transports 27.2 to the object named in the S2 frontier.
Using `offOrdProjectiveDefect_eq_half_reflection_tsum`, the off-ordinate projective
defect is one half of the sum of the symmetrized summands `offPairTerm`, the near
off-ordinate set `nearOffFinset t J` is finite, and

    | D_off(g, t, r) - (1/2) * sum over nearOffFinset t J of offPairTerm |
        <= (1/2) * C * farShellBound A |t| J
        (`abs_offOrdProjectiveDefect_sub_nearCore_le`),

with `C` a uniform curvature bound for the *projected* taper `projTaper g r`.  The
packaged forms `exists_offOrd_near_far_split` and `exists_cutoff_offOrd_near_far_split`
supply `A`, `C` and the cutoff.

### 27.3 What this does and does not settle

It converts the infinite part of S2 into a mechanical payment: for any accuracy the far
shells can be discharged by choosing `J`.  It does **not** supply the cancellation.  The
first unproved theorem is now the *finite* one:

> A signed lower bound on the near-shell core: for a constructed three-taper family `g`
> at some radius `r`, some cutoff `J`, and uniformly over off-line targets, an estimate
> of the finite signed sum `∑_{ρ ∈ nearFinset t J} (Z_ρ + Z_{Rρ})` strong enough that,
> together with the far remainder `C · farShellBound A |t| J` of §27.2, it yields
>
>     ‖D_off(g, t, r)‖² ≤ B_far < det3(D_Π, D_Γ, D_cluster)² / wedgeSq(D_Π, D_Γ).

§25.3 remains in force: the inequality cannot come from the elimination algebra, since
`det3(D_Π, D_Γ, D_cluster) = det3(D_Π, D_Γ, D_off)`; it must come from genuine signed
cancellation, now confined to finitely many shells.

**The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.**  `RiemannHypothesisFor` is untouched and
unweakened; nothing in this pass asserts or assumes it.

## §28 The finite core, scalarized: determinant taper and local zero phase sum

Two new modules replace the vector-valued finite near-core problem of §27.3 by a
single scalar one, and record the negative result that reflection symmetry cannot
contribute to the cancellation.

### 28.1 The exact bilinear elimination form and the finite Gram ledger

`ThreeTaperSchurGram` polarizes the surviving-energy identity of §24:

    ⟪E x, E y⟫ = det3(n₁,n₂,x) · det3(n₁,n₂,y) / wedgeSq(n₁,n₂)
        (`dotP_elim2_eq_det3_mul_det3_div`),

`E = elim2 n₁ n₂` being the two-nuisance elimination.  Consequences, all exact:

* `normSqP_elim2_sum_eq_diag_add_gramDebt` — for a finite family `v` on a `Finset s`,

      ‖E (∑ v i)‖² = ∑ ‖E (v i)‖² + gramDebt,

  with `gramDebt` the fully signed off-diagonal sum: no absolute values, no
  cardinality factor.
* `normSqP_elim2_sum_eq_sq_sum_cellDet_div` and `gramDebt_eq_sq_sum_sub_sum_sq_div` —
  with `d i = det3(n₁,n₂,v i)`,

      ‖E (∑ v i)‖² = (∑ d i)² / wedgeSq,     gramDebt = ((∑ d)² − ∑ d²) / wedgeSq.

* `dotP_elim2_of_eq_nonneg` (**no-go**) — equal cells pair to `+‖E v‖² ≥ 0`.  The near
  cell of an off-ordinate zero is *already* the reflection-symmetrized response
  `Z_σ + Z_{Rσ}`, so the members indexed by `σ` and by `Rσ` are literally equal:
  reflection produces duplicates, never negative Gram debt.  Reflection has already
  spent itself in killing the odd `sinh(a u) sin(δ u)` channel.

### 28.2 The literal finite core is one oscillatory integral

`NearCoreDeterminantTaper` carries the scalarization onto the literal carrier.  With
`c = n₁ × n₂`, define the **determinant taper** and the **local zero phase sum**

    q(u) = ∑ⱼ cⱼ · projTaper gⱼ r u                                   (`detTaper`)
    S(t,J;u) = ∑_{σ ∈ nearOffFinset t J} m_σ cosh(a_σ u) cos((b_σ − t) u)
                                                             (`localZeroPhaseSum`).

Then, for compactly supported even `C²` tapers:

* `cellDet_nearCell_eq_integral` — one zero's scalar cell response is
  `2 m_σ ∫ q(u) cosh(a_σ u) cos((b_σ − t)u) du`;
* `sum_cellDet_nearCell_eq_integral` — the whole finite near core is the single
  integral `2 ∫ q(u) S(t,J;u) du`;
* `normSqP_elim2_nearVec_eq` — hence `‖E D_near(J)‖² = 4 (∫ q S)² / wedgeSq(n₁,n₂)`;
* `integral_detTaper_localZeroPhaseSum_eq_sum` — the same scalar as a finite
  exponential sum `∑_σ m_σ · Q(a_σ, b_σ − t)` over the near zeros
  (`zeroDetResponse`);
* `abs_det3_offVec_sub_det3_nearVec_le` and `normSqP_elim2_offVec_le` — the far
  remainder of §27.2b transported into the same scalar coordinate:

      ‖E D_off‖² ≤ (|2 ∫ q S| + ‖c‖₁ · ½ C · farShellBound A |t| J)² / wedgeSq(n₁,n₂);

* `normSqP_elim2_offVec_lt_of_scalar_bound` — the consumer form: a scalar bound
  strictly below `wedgeSq · M` gives `‖E D_off‖² < M`.  The scalar bound is a
  *hypothesis* there; nothing in this pass asserts it.

### 28.3 The first unproved theorem, stated exactly

> For a constructed three-taper family `g` at some radius `r`, deterministic nuisance
> directions `n₁, n₂` with `wedgeSq n₁ n₂ > 0`, some cutoff `J ≥ 1`, and uniformly over
> off-line targets `t`: a bound for the target-centred local zero exponential sum
>
>     ∫ q(u) · ∑_{σ ∈ nearOffFinset t J} m_σ cosh(a_σ u) cos((b_σ − t) u) du
>
> strong enough that
>
>     (|2 ∫ q S| + ‖c‖₁ · ½ C · farShellBound A |t| J)² < wedgeSq(n₁,n₂) · M_cluster.

No generic Gram algebra, no Schur geometry, no convergence question and no reflection
bookkeeping stands in the way of that statement any more: §28.1 and §28.2 discharge all
of them.  What remains is genuine harmonic analysis — cancellation among the phases
`cos((b_σ − t)u)` of the finitely many zeros in the window `|Im σ − t| < J`.  Note the
constraint of §25.3 still applies to any route that hopes to end in a contradiction:
`det3(D_Π, D_Γ, D_cluster) = det3(D_Π, D_Γ, D_off)` under the literal projective
balance, so the strict inequality cannot be produced by the elimination algebra.

**The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.**  `RiemannHypothesisFor` is untouched and
unweakened; nothing in this pass asserts or assumes it.

## 29. The pole-quotient complement margin: the exact shape of what is left

`Zeta23Bridge/PoleQuotientComplementMargin.lean` (all statements machine-checked,
axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only).

### 29.1 The contradiction compiler, and only that

With `D_cluster = ℓ(Q_cluster)`, `D_off = ℓ(Q_offOrd)`, `D_Γ = ℓ(Q_Γ)` read by the
even-cone functional of §21:

* `evenCone_three_channel_balance` — once the parity quotient kills the pole
  channel (`poleEvenResp g t r = 0`) and the short taper kills the prime channel
  (`primeVec = 0`), the literal Weil balance is exactly `D_cluster + D_off + D_Γ = 0`;
* `false_of_split_budget` / `false_of_evenCone_split_budget` — budgets
  `|D_off| ≤ B_off`, `|D_Γ| ≤ B_Γ` together with `M ≤ D_cluster` and
  `B_off + B_Γ < M` are contradictory.  Ordered algebra only; no estimate is
  manufactured;
* `clusterValue_le_budget` — the inequality the compiler contradicts,
  `D_cluster ≤ B_off + B_Γ`, holds for every taper of the lane.

### 29.2 The margin cannot be height-free

For a nonnegative taper supported inside the sampling window `2r|u| < π/2`:

* `evenResp_zero_le_evenResp` — the even response is monotone in the height,
  `A_0(r) ≤ A_a(r)`;
* `baseline_le_budget` — hence at *any* zero of the target ordinate, on the
  critical line or off it, `2 A_0(r) ≤ D_cluster ≤ B_off + B_Γ`;
* `no_zero_at_ordinate_of_heightFree_margin` (**no-go**) — a strict margin based
  on the height-free quantity `2 A_0(r)` would exclude every zero at that
  ordinate, including on-line ones.  The strict comparison must be paid out of
  the height surplus `A_a(r) − A_0(r)` and nothing else.

This is the even-cone analogue of the §28.1 reflection no-go and of the projective
balance no-go: it removes a whole family of would-be final estimates.

### 29.3 How much surplus there is: an explicit window

* `heightSurplus_le` — `A_a(r) − A_0(r) ≤ (cosh(|a|Λ) − 1) ∫ g` for a taper
  supported in `|u| ≤ Λ`;
* `taperMass_le_evenResp_zero` — inside the window `∫ g ≤ √2 A_0(r)`;
* `evenResp_le_baseline_mul` — hence `A_a(r) ≤ A_0(r)(1 + √2(cosh(|a|Λ) − 1))`;
* `baselineCluster`, `baselineCluster_le_clusterValue`,
  `clusterValue_le_baselineCluster_mul` — the same at fibre level, with
  `baselineCluster = ∑ 2 m_ρ A_0(r)`;
* `cosh_sub_one_le_sq` and `poleQuotientMarginWindow_quantitative` — for the
  high-ordinate taper of `exists_positive_taper_poleEven_zero` (supported in
  `|u| < 9π/(4|t|)`, and `|a| ≤ 1/2` on the strip),

      baselineCluster ≤ B_off + B_Γ,
      D_cluster ≤ baselineCluster · (1 + √2 (9π/(8|t|))²);

* `complementChannels_pinned` — with no budget hypothesis at all, the negated sum
  `−(D_off + D_Γ)` lies in that same window.

### 29.4 The first unproved theorem, stated exactly

> For the high-ordinate taper `g` of `exists_positive_taper_poleEven_zero` at a
> nontrivial zero of `riemannZeta` of ordinate `t` with `9π ≤ 4|t| log 2`:
> budgets `B_off ≥ |ℓ(Q_offOrd)|` and `B_Γ ≥ |ℓ(Q_Γ)|` with
>
>     B_off + B_Γ < 2 m_{ρ*} A_{a_*}(r),   a_* = Re ρ* − 1/2 ≠ 0.
>
> By §29.3 any such pair must satisfy
>
>     baselineCluster ≤ B_off + B_Γ < baselineCluster (1 + √2 (9π/(8|t|))²),
>
> i.e. the two complement channels have to be evaluated to *relative* accuracy
> `O(|t|^{-2})`, not merely bounded.  The owned envelopes `epsZero` (built from
> the whole off-ordinate weight `W(t)`) and `epsGamma` are finite but are nowhere
> near that accuracy, and no argument in this repository produces such an
> evaluation.

**The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.**  `RiemannHypothesisFor` is untouched and
unweakened; nothing in this pass asserts or assumes it.

### 29.5 `B_Γ`: a gamma budget linear in the taper strip constant

`Zeta23Bridge/PoleQuotientGammaBudget.lean` factorises the gamma envelope.  With
`|ĝ(x + s − t)| ≤ stripConst(k_s, Λ)/(1 + x²)` (companion `[eq:hfbound]` at a real
point) and `|B(x)| ≤ A + B√|x|` (companion Stirling clause), the `x`-integral
splits into the taper's strip constant times the absolute number

    cΓ = ∫ (A + B√|x|)/(1 + x²) dx        (`gammaTailConstant`, `integrable_gammaMajorant`),

giving `exists_gamma_budget_linear_in_stripConst`:

    ∃ CΓ ≥ 0 (absolute), ∀ taper, ordinate, radius:
      |ℓ(Q_Γ)| ≤ CΓ · (stripConst(k_r, Λ) + stripConst(k_{−r}, Λ)).

This is the literal `B_Γ` of the lane, uniform in `t` and `r`.  It is *not* small:
`stripConst(k, Λ) = e^{Λ/2}(‖k‖₁ + ‖k''‖₁)`, and the second-derivative term of the
high-ordinate taper grows as its support shrinks, so this budget does not meet the
relative `O(|t|^{-2})` accuracy demanded by §29.3–29.4.  No claim to the contrary
is made anywhere.

## 30. `M_cluster^pole`: the margin instantiated, and the window from both sides

`Zeta23Bridge/PoleQuotientClusterMargin.lean` (all statements machine-checked,
axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only).

§29 bounded the height surplus of the cluster from *above* and showed the margin
of the split-budget compiler cannot be height-free.  It left open whether the
admissible interval for the two complement budgets is non-empty at all.  It is,
and its width is exactly of the same quadratic order as the upper bound.

### 30.1 The height surplus is exactly quadratic

For a nonnegative taper supported inside the sampling window `2r|u| < π/2`:

* `one_add_sq_div_two_le_cosh` — `1 + x²/2 ≤ cosh x` (from `cosh 2y = 1 + 2 sinh²y`
  and `y ≤ sinh y`);
* `heightSurplus_ge` —

      A_a(r) − A_0(r) ≥ (√2/4) a² ∫ g(u) u² du,

  the quantitative counterpart of `heightSurplus_le`;
* `heightSurplus_two_sided` — both bounds together,

      (√2/4) a² ∫ g u² ≤ A_a(r) − A_0(r) ≤ (cosh(|a|Λ) − 1) ∫ g.

Since `cosh(|a|Λ) − 1 ≤ (aΛ)²` for `|a|Λ ≤ 1` (§29.3), the height surplus is of
order `(aΛ)²` from both sides: no reformulation of the taper inside this lane can
make the surplus larger than quadratic, and none can make it smaller either.

### 30.2 The socket `M_cluster^pole`

* `secondMoment`, `secondMoment_nonneg` — `∫ g(u) u² du`;
* `clusterMargin g ρ = (√2/2) (Re ρ − 1/2)² ∫ g u²`, with
  `clusterMargin_pos_of_offLine`: strictly positive as soon as the zero is off the
  critical line and the taper has positive second moment;
* `clusterValue_ge_baseline_add_margin` — **the socket, instantiated**:

      baselineCluster + clusterMargin(ρ_*) ≤ ℓ(Q_cluster)

  for *any* zero `ρ_*` of the target ordinate.  The proof is fibre-wise: every
  term of `ℓ(Q_cluster) − baselineCluster` is nonnegative (height monotonicity),
  so the whole difference dominates the single term of `ρ_*`, which is at least
  `2 · (√2/4) a_*² ∫ g u²` by §30.1;
* `budget_exceeds_baseline_plus_margin` — unconditionally, every admissible pair
  of complement budgets satisfies `baselineCluster + clusterMargin(ρ_*) ≤ B_off + B_Γ`;
* `marginWindow_two_sided` — the two constraints together.

### 30.3 What this changes, and what it does not

The quantitative margin socket of the pole-quotient lane is now **closed**: the
final comparison no longer needs a new lower bound for the cluster, only budgets
accurate to within `clusterMargin(ρ_*)` in absolute terms.  For the high-ordinate
taper (`Λ ≤ 9π/(4|t|)`, `|a_*| ≤ 1/2`) that absolute accuracy is of order
`a_*² Λ² ∫ g`, i.e. `O(|t|^{-2})` relative to the baseline — the same order as the
upper bound of §29.3, so the demand of §29.4 is sharp and cannot be relaxed by any
choice of taper within the lane.

The first unproved theorem is unchanged in substance and is still the off-ordinate
budget: an estimate `|ℓ(Q_offOrd)| ≤ B_off` with

    B_off + B_Γ < baselineCluster + (√2/2) a_*² ∫ g u².

`B_Γ` of §29.5 is proved but far too large for this window, and no argument in this
repository produces an off-ordinate estimate of that accuracy.

**The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.**  `RiemannHypothesisFor` is untouched and
unweakened.

### 30.4 The margin, measured against the baseline

* `secondMoment_le_sq_mul_mass` — `∫ g u² ≤ Λ² ∫ g` for a taper supported in
  `|u| ≤ Λ`;
* `clusterMargin_le_sq_mul_baseline` — hence, inside the sampling window,

      clusterMargin(ρ) ≤ a² Λ² · A_0(r),   a = Re ρ − 1/2,

  so the margin is at most `a² Λ²` relative to the height-free baseline response.
  For the high-ordinate taper (`Λ ≤ 9π/(4|t|)`, `|a| ≤ 1/2`) this is
  `≤ (9π/(8|t|))² / 4`: the prose estimate of §29.3–§29.4 is now itself a checked
  theorem, not a remark.

## 31. Which budgets can complete the lane, and how they must be obtained

`Zeta23Bridge/PoleQuotientBudgetCircularity.lean` (machine-checked, standard
axioms only).

### 31.1 The inequality

For every correct pair of budgets the balance gives

    B_off + B_Γ ≥ |D_off| + |D_Γ| ≥ |D_off + D_Γ| = D_cluster    (`cluster_le_budget_sum`).

Three consequences, all machine-checked:

* `not_strict_margin_of_budgets` — the exact contrapositive of the compiler:
  from the balance, correct budgets and a margin `M ≤ D_cluster`, the strict
  comparison `B_off + B_Γ < M` is false.  This is not a refutation of the lane; it
  is the statement that, inside the context of a hypothetical off-line zero, a
  proof of the strict comparison *is* the contradiction, i.e. is a proof of the
  Riemann Hypothesis.  Nothing here shows such a proof exists, and nothing here
  shows it cannot;
* `not_strict_margin_of_signed_budgets` — one-sided budgets change nothing: the
  compiler only uses the two lower bounds;
* `not_strict_margin_with_exact_gamma`, `not_strict_margin_with_exact_offOrdinate`
  — no budget can be smaller than the exact channel value.  So the obligation of
  §29.4 is not "sharpen `B_Γ`" but "evaluate `ℓ(Q_offOrd)` and `ℓ(Q_Γ)` to within
  the cluster margin".

### 31.2 The methodological constraint

The inequality above is a constraint on *how* the budgets may be derived: any
estimate that routes through the balance — through `D_off + D_Γ = −D_cluster`, or
through any quantity already bounded below by `D_cluster` — reproduces
`B_off + B_Γ ≥ D_cluster` and cannot give the strict comparison.  The two budgets
must come from an independent analysis: the off-ordinate zero sum through its own
summation, and the gamma bracket through Stirling, as in §29.5.

`budget_sum_ge_baseline_add_margin` records the resulting pin: every correct
budget sum is at least `baselineCluster + (√2/2) a_*² ∫ g u²`.

### 31.3 The frontier is unchanged

The first unproved theorem is still the one recorded in §29.4 and §30.3: an
off-ordinate estimate accurate to the cluster margin, together with a gamma
estimate of the same accuracy.  §30 shows this accuracy demand is sharp — the
available surplus is of order `(a_* Λ)²` from both sides — and §31 shows it cannot
be circumvented by any reformulation of the budgets themselves.

**The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.**  `RiemannHypothesisFor` is untouched and
unweakened.
